#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130306} :activity/id #uuid "69de34ab-1f09-5179-b87a-f14938999f8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050603 :topic/id #uuid "579b98fc-d572-5a6a-9016-eb840c350a9d" :topic/math-academy-id 2017} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130402 :tutorial/content "How do we construct a particular rotation-scaling matrix $A?$\n\nFirst, recall that rotation matrices are of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the matrix $R$ rotates a vector by an angle of $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nScaling matrices are of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe scaling matrix $S$ scales a vector by a scale factor of $r,$ where the origin is the center of enlargement. \n\nWe can write any rotation-scaling matrix $A$ as a product of a scaling matrix and a rotation matrix: \n\n$$\nA=SR\n$$\n\nOr, more explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a451bc3-5ec6-543a-911f-04a39b6fdcf4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4390 :tutorial/title "Constructing a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4206234b-6f29-5edd-8f26-20b644723a7d" :step/math-academy-id 26900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130322 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf A$ by its standard matrix $A,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{A}$ by computing the matrix products as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=i+2\\,j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b562af85ac2ad0f4871105d61b43aa8e27d215a4006c0ee3371cd673b2dd9579.png)\n\nThe transformation rotates the square by an angle of \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}1}\\right) \\approx 63.4^\\circ,\n$$\n\nthen scales it by a scale factor of \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{{\\color{blue}1}^2 + {\\color{red}2}^2} = \\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nIn fact, any matrix of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ and $\\color{red}b$ are real numbers (not both equal to zero simultaneously) is a matrix that rotates and then scales. \nThese types of matrices are called **rotation-scaling matrices**." :tutorial/id #uuid "81772221-4f86-554a-a3a0-b51dc4c6d12e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2012 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4570acd4-fcc3-52d3-a60f-f7dacdc74dad" :step/math-academy-id 7464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129321 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128885 :question/id #uuid "c9af0fcb-c124-58f9-b8df-76db9b1c1520" :question/math-academy-id "e-2174"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359bc41e-1b6d-5177-9642-4f8697bc5ba0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351309}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation-Scaling Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "fd76e3d2-8f30-54e7-b718-5d23d3d0401f" :step/math-academy-id 7465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130323 :tutorial/content "We've seen that we can write a rotation-scaling matrix $A$ is the product of a rotation and a scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the natural question now is, how does a rotation-scaling matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nrelate to the individual rotation and scaling matrices?\n\nFirst, setting the two equal to each other, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find the scale factor and angle of rotation of a rotation-scaling matrix. First, notice that we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo find $r$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we can square and then add the two equations above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the scale factor $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} = \\sqrt{\\det{A}}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the rotation angle $\\theta$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we start with the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{b}{a}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac ba \\right).\n$$\n\nIt's also possible to recover both the original scaling *and* rotation matrix once the scale factor $r$ is known. Let's see how that works." :tutorial/id #uuid "1fb2c1d9-0856-55ca-945a-8e01f2e58902" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2014 :tutorial/title "Finding the Scale Factor and Rotation Angle Of a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e43d8235-fa21-5652-b196-42d8e706cde2" :step/math-academy-id 7473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129322 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128886 :question/id #uuid "9e92a0a5-0876-5767-ae40-b791fac08a2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5304084-5020-5bac-afe0-a4eaf6f0272d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402635}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factors and Angles of Rotation of Rotation-Scaling Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "1cd1b6e2-db31-5859-8ab0-4539c67bdc7c" :step/math-academy-id 7474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129323 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128887 :question/id #uuid "c2717347-4d98-5e51-81d5-1cacc7d8f754" :question/math-academy-id "e-2181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c15923f7-ddc0-5012-9645-922ef36cce97" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394263}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Image of a Vector Under the Action Of a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "25e203cd-b5fa-517f-8b98-1bad7223ac86" :step/math-academy-id 7485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129324 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128890 :question/id #uuid "ce7914f3-83d3-5da6-9f93-a31a0ff386ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-2182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71ea2c0c-8bcb-5674-a938-4357302d6429" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394235}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Rotation-Scaling Matrix That Represents a Geometric Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "8615c849-580e-5043-ba3f-ab9b5733b43c" :step/math-academy-id 7489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130308}}] :activity/title "Rotation-Scaling Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129360}]}
